  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/0432f45ba5b0652edc7f620233e9d2095c6e6836e98cecddf9ebc6f8c4cf99a5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/91d605a297f2fd3c90c59592cc08184c4201f04ebc69bfc285e4261af8e918d2.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.9,\\,\\rho ≈0.1,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $-1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.8.$",
